@@s001 Linear transformations in 2D
@@s002 dato:u and v,due:T(u) and T(v),ok:T(u + v),no:non-linear function
@@s003 dato:e₁ and its image,due:e₂ and its image,ok:T(x)
@@s004 dato:image of e₁ (first column),due:image of e₂ (second column),ora:the transformed square
@@s005 The unit square and the two vectors of the standard basis: <b>e₁ = [1, 0]</b> and <b>e₂ = [0, 1]</b>. The identity matrix changes nothing.
@@s006 spento:unit square (area 1),ora:image,no:zero area
@@s007 spento:starting square,ora:after the first transformation,ok:result
@@s008 dato:x,ora:Ax,ok:A⁻¹(Ax) = x,no:no way back
@@s009 dato:two different inputs,no:same output,spento:possible outputs
@@s010 spento:starting square,no:translated,ora:the origin
@@s011 Linear transformations in 2D
@@s012 Multiplying by a matrix <b>transforms</b> space: it stretches it, rotates it, reflects it, shears it. Here: the definition of linear transformation, why the columns of the matrix say everything, the catalogue of transformations, the determinant as area, composition, the inverse, the "one-to-one" and "onto" properties, and why translation stays out. Weeks 3 and 4.
@@s013 What makes a transformation linear
@@s014 A <b>transformation</b> (or <i>mapping</i>) T takes a vector and returns another. It is a vector-valued function, like those of week 1, but seen through the eyes of geometry: what it does to the points of the plane.
@@s015 Definition
@@s016 T is <b>linear</b> if for every vector u, v and number c: <span class="m">T(u + v) = T(u) + T(v)</span> (additivity) and <span class="m">T(c·u) = c·T(u)</span> (homogeneity). Consequence: <span class="m">T(0) = 0</span>, the origin stays still.
@@s017 In words: adding and stretching <b>before</b> or <b>after</b> the transformation gives the same result. Geometrically straight lines remain straight lines, parallel lines remain parallel, and the centre does not move.
@@s018 The fundamental fact: every linear transformation from ℝⁿ to ℝᵐ is written as <span class="m">T(x) = Ax</span> for a matrix A (m × n), and every matrix defines a linear transformation. Matrices and linear transformations are the same thing.
@@s019 T(u + v) = T(u) + T(v), and a function that does not satisfy it
@@s020 The columns are the images of the basis
@@s021 How is the matrix of a transformation found? Just ask <b>where the vectors of the standard basis go</b>, e₁ = [1, 0] and e₂ = [0, 1]. Their images, written as columns, <b>are</b> the matrix.
@@s022 Why the columns are the images of the basis
@@s023 Every vector is a linear combination of the standard basis.
@@s024 By linearity.
@@s025 It is the column view of <a href="w03-matrici.html">matrix times vector</a>.
@@s026 It is one of the most powerful ideas of the course: a linear transformation is completely determined by what it does to a few vectors (as many as the dimension). In week 5, to learn an affine transformation from data, three point correspondences are enough.
@@s027 Where e₁ and e₂ go, everything goes
@@s028 The catalogue: scaling, rotation, reflection, shear, projection
@@s029 The linear transformations of the plane are recognised by what they do to the <b>unit square</b> (the square with sides e₁ and e₂): it always becomes a parallelogram, possibly squashed.
@@s030 Transformation
@@s031 Matrix
@@s032 det
@@s033 scaling by sₓ, s<sub>y</sub>
@@s034 rotation by φ (anticlockwise)
@@s035 [[cos φ, −sin φ], [sin φ, cos φ]]
@@s036 reflection in the y axis
@@s037 horizontal shear
@@s038 projection onto the x axis
@@s039 0 (not invertible)
@@s040 For the rotation: with φ = 90° one has cos φ = 0 and sin φ = 1, and the matrix becomes [[0, −1], [1, 0]]. All these matrices are read column by column: the first column is where e₁ goes, the second where e₂ goes.
@@s041 What the matrices do to the unit square
@@s042 The determinant: how the area changes
@@s043 The unit square has area 1; after the transformation A it becomes a parallelogram of area <b>|det A|</b>. The determinant is the factor by which the transformation multiplies <b>all</b> the areas.
@@s044 The sign adds information: negative means the transformation <b>flips</b> the orientation (like seeing the plane in a mirror). And det = 0 means the plane is squashed onto a line or a point: the area disappears, information is lost, and the transformation cannot be inverted. It is the same determinant of <a href="w03-sistemi-lineari.html">linear systems</a>, seen in motion.
@@s045 Scaling, rotation, shear, reflection, projection: the areas
@@s046 Composing transformations: order matters
@@s047 Applying first a transformation R and then another S corresponds to multiplying by the product <span class="m">SR</span>: x → Rx → S(Rx) = (SR)x. The matrix applied <b>first</b> sits on the <b>right</b>, attached to x, like the inner function in f(g(x)).
@@s048 First rotate, then scale
@@s049 The transformation applied <b>first</b> sits on the <b>right</b>, next to x.
@@s050 Different: rotating and then scaling horizontally is not the same as scaling and then rotating. (Computed with NumPy.)
@@s051 A chain of transformations thus reduces to a single matrix, the product of all of them, computed once and applied to many points. It is also the principle of neural networks, which compose many transformations (with non-linear functions in between).
@@s052 SR and RS on the same square
@@s053 Inverting a transformation
@@s054 The <b>inverse</b> A⁻¹ undoes the transformation: A⁻¹(Ax) = x for every x. Geometrically it is immediately clear how it is made: the rotation by 90° is undone by the rotation by −90°, the scaling by 2 by the scaling by ½, a shear to the right by a shear to the left.
@@s055 Not every transformation can be undone. If A squashes the plane (det = 0), different points end up in the same point, and from the result the origin cannot be reconstructed. For orthogonal matrices (rotations and reflections) the inverse is simply the transpose.
@@s056 In ML it serves to go back: for example from the screen coordinates to the camera coordinates, or from the basis of the PCA to the original one.
@@s057 R⁻¹ brings back, P does not
@@s058 Injective and surjective (one-to-one and onto)
@@s059 Two properties that the exercises <i>Linear Transformations One-to-One and Onto</i> of week 3 ask you to recognise.
@@s060 <b>Injective</b> (<i>one-to-one</i>): different inputs always give different outputs, nothing is "overlapped". For a matrix it means that the <b>columns are linearly independent</b>, and that the only vector sent to 0 is 0.
@@s061 <b>Surjective</b> (<i>onto</i>): every vector of the target space is the image of something. For a matrix it means that the columns <b>generate</b> (span) the whole target space.
@@s062 Counting the dimensions helps. An m × n matrix goes from ℝⁿ to ℝᵐ. If n &gt; m (more inputs than outputs, "wide") it cannot be injective: something must overlap. If n &lt; m ("tall") it cannot be surjective: n columns are not enough to generate ℝᵐ. For a square matrix the two properties coincide, and hold exactly when det ≠ 0, that is when the matrix is invertible.
@@s063 The projection: neither injective nor surjective
@@s064 Translation is not linear
@@s065 Moving all the points by the same vector t, x ↦ x + t, is a <b>translation</b>. It is very useful (to superimpose two poses you must also move them), but it is not linear: it moves the origin, whereas every linear transformation leaves it still (A·0 = 0 for every matrix A). So no 2 × 2 matrix does a translation.
@@s066 So what?
@@s067 It is recovered with <b>affine transformations</b> <span class="m">Ax + t</span> and with <b>homogeneous coordinates</b>, which, by adding a component 1 to every vector, make the translation a product of matrices too: <a href="w05-affine-omogenee.html">affine and homogeneous coordinates</a>.
@@s068 Moving everything by [1, 1] moves the origin too
